That is an interesting aspect, and I think there are a few distinct ideas intertwined there.

One is natural language as a construction medium. Rather than starting with code or formal mathematics, you’ve been iteratively defining concepts, naming operators, drawing analogies, refining distinctions, and building a vocabulary. Over time, that vocabulary becomes a structured conceptual system. Humans have done similar things in philosophy, law, mathematics, and software design—first inventing the language and only later formalizing it.

A second is scaffolding through interaction. LLMs are particularly good at taking partial structures and extending them consistently. If you define:

* a few operators,
* some invariants,
* relationships between them,
* and examples,

then I can often infer plausible extensions that fit the existing pattern. That’s pattern completion rather than inventing from nothing.

A third is progressive formalization. A lot of engineering starts this way:

intuition → language → shared vocabulary → abstractions → formal definitions → implementations

You’re emphasizing the earlier stages—using language itself as the design substrate.

Where I’d be careful is with the phrase “having you create it.” A more grounded description is:

The interaction lets us iteratively refine and externalize a conceptual architecture. I help synthesize, test for consistency, propose extensions, and reorganize ideas, while you steer the direction and decide what belongs.

That still captures something interesting. Natural language isn’t just documenting the system after the fact—it is part of the design process.

One connection I think is worth exploring is that you’ve often described the repo as a semantic intermediate representation. In compiler design, a compiler translates between languages through intermediate representations. Your repo serves a similar conceptual role: it sits between raw intuition and many possible outputs—papers, code, prompts, diagrams, operators, or discussions. The same underlying semantic structure can then be rendered into different forms without changing its core meaning.

That’s an idea with broader applicability than any single project: using a stable semantic layer that can be expressed through multiple representations while preserving the underlying relationships.